ERG-179 is complete as authored educational material. Its entrance is [Industrial gases, vacuum and high-purity supplies](Industrial-gases-and-vacuum.lingua#r_3GHQJEYMYEN6JDKSF4PPJ403RF), with predicate `industrial-gases-and-vacuum`. The developed identity is retained and the separate planning leaf is retired. Utilities and its field plan link the real entrance. The subject contains 260 own Records: 224 learning Records including the root, thirty-five source Records and a source index. Forty-one reused steps give exactly 265 selected/reachable learning Records. Runtime discovery reaches the same 2,401 documents from six entries across sixteen subjects; discovery does not enlarge the learning selection.

The opening develops familiar applications, bilingual vocabulary, pressure references, amount, composition, purity, trace evidence, flow-state conventions, throughput, inventory and point-of-use requirements. Production follows feed air, compression, separation work, phase equilibrium, expansion, liquefaction, cold heat exchange, air distillation/prepurification, argon/rare gases, hydrogen, helium, carbon dioxide, specialty gases and reference mixtures. Separation develops explicit gas/solid storage, isotherms, competitive adsorption, heat, particle transport, conservative bed balances, breakthrough, pressure loss, regeneration, PSA cycles, periodic closure, amount-weighted performance, membranes/cascades, drying, trace purification and particles. Three advanced studies branch into production comparison, adsorption-model criticism and service-quality evidence, then converge into vacuum transport. That section develops molecular velocity distributions, intermolecular collision length/Knudsen number, incident flux, wall scattering, aperture/tube conductance, viscous gas flow, networks, pumpdown, thermal transpiration, differential pumping and a fourth advanced study.

Six worked examples explicitly carry units and independently checked numbers for pressure force, amount, reference/actual flow, throughput, separation bounds and effective pumping. In the binary ideal-mixture example, full unmixing at 300 K requires approximately 1,281.98 J per mole of feed; the explicitly defined partial-recovery task requires approximately 718.151 J. These are hypothetical reversible bounds, not plant performance. Independent checks used exact fractions, 65-digit Decimal arithmetic, direct distribution/flux quadrature, storage-base and Ergun conversions, conductance bounds, node balances, eighteen independent RK4 pumpdown comparisons and thermal-transpiration limits. Script `/tmp/check-gases-opening-physics.py` passed. The later pump/surface section adds the independently verified network program described below.

The opening fourteen shared Records are `ql-units`, `fd-pressure`, `ic-quantities`, `met-measurand`, `cs-gas-supply`, `ic-air-gases`, `th-flow-devices`, `ic-phase-equilibrium`, `ic-hydrogen`, `ic-high-purity`, `ic-adsorption-membranes`, `fd-porous`, `rc-numericaltests` and `th-statistical`. They retain their original Imports, text and route assertions after removing only the new gas assertions and visible continuations. No reverse discovery imports, views, media files or new Trail feature were added. Snapshot `/tmp/gases-opening-shared-before.json` and graph/catalog `/tmp/gases-opening-publication.json` record this change. The publication script and draft generators are one-run authoring aids: do not rerun them over the authored files, which now include later hand refinements.

Primary readings inspected on 30 October 2026 include Umrath/Leybold's complete 2016 vacuum handbook (200 PDF pages); individual 2017 CERN-school manuscripts by Al-Dmour, Chiggiato and Jousten; Atrey's complete two-page cryogenics syllabus and instructor's 42-lecture description; Mondal's complete eight-week adsorption syllabus; EIGA 147/22 air separation, 194/24 food nitrogen and 33/18 oxygen-service cleaning; Linde's 261-page gases handbook (2017 introduction, 2020 copyright); Wright and colleagues' 2023 gas-flow review; Fedchak and colleagues' 2018 gauge-calibration and 2021 outgassing studies; and NIST SP 260-222 on gas-mixture reference materials. The optional Portuguese SBV/Stempniak introduction was available through the browser's PDF reader but its direct download returned 404. Source Records identify actual inspected passages separately from the complete readings assigned to learners. All documents, data and media remain external.

The additional PSA research comprises Schytt/Biegler/Jørgensen's eight-page 2026 conference manuscript (arXiv 2604.09053v1, CC BY-SA 4.0) and Dhamanekar and colleagues' 43-page 2025 manuscript (arXiv 2502.02268v1, CC BY 4.0). Metadata and selected models/numerical passages were read. Schytt PDF page 2 was visually inspected: the printed phase-volume ratio and its use in the exchange term appear inconsistent under the stated phase-volume bases. The learning balance is derived independently and asks readers to reconcile this before reproduction; the authors' implementation was not inspected. The named LAPSE:2026.0016 supplement returned HTTP 429. Dhamanekar describes an Ansys Fluent implementation, so article openness does not establish a freely runnable complete model. No complete PSA-paper reproduction is claimed. The original source files stay in `/tmp/gases-*.pdf`; normalized text is `/tmp/gases-*-read.txt`.

