New primary sources are Baglin/Kersevan's 2024 *Vacuum systems*, an 80-page JUAS chapter with exercises/solutions (DOI 10.23730/CYRSP-2024-003.1259, CC BY 4.0); Chiggiato's 19-page *Vacuum Technology for Superconducting Devices*, CERN-2014-005 with 2015 author version (arXiv 1501.07162v1, CC BY 3.0); and Taborelli's 11-page 2025 *Surface Finishing and Coatings for Accelerator Vacuum Applications* (arXiv 2506.23691v1, arXiv distribution licence). Metadata, contents and selected pumping, heat-transfer, cryogenic, outgassing, preparation/adhesion and getter passages were inspected. Leybold and the existing outgassing sources were read further. Fedchak's fixture/conductance/uncertainty passages and figure 4 on PDF page 17 were inspected, including visual inspection of the pressure-rise plot. The paper states supporting data are within the article; a separate complete raw-time-series download was not established. No full experimental reproduction is claimed. The CERN 2006 getter-production preprint returned an access challenge; the associated Prodromides thesis now has restricted files at CERN, and its EPFL link was inaccessible. These were not promoted into usable full-reading source Records. The accessible newer chapters provide the current readings. All media remain external.

The original standard-library Python program in `computation/An-inspectable-network-pumpdown-and-gas-balance.lingua` solves constant-temperature molecular-flow networks with constant nonnegative loads and linear pump speeds using backward Euler and a reusable Cholesky factorization. It returns pressure histories, injected/removed inventory and a balance residual. Sixteen chambers and 20,000 steps bound the example. Validation rejects invalid domains and reports unreliable floating-point updates rather than clipping them. It does not claim to model variable pumping, finite compression, adsorption, heat or capacity exhaustion. The source was extracted from the actual Record and executed in fresh Python processes from `/tmp`. SHA-256 is `571497bd9dfb2fa86898d0cf67baede2a7d345319559a91bb8998a7d890a1cae`. Fifty-seven exact discrete/analytical or independent 70-digit Gaussian-elimination comparisons passed, including conservation and index permutations, with nineteen invalid cases, zero-step and nonmutation checks. Continuous-solution time refinements produced errors approximately 0.0251455, 0.0128029, 0.00646076 and 0.00324544, showing the expected first order. Scripts `/tmp/check-gases-rendered-network.py` and `/tmp/gases-rendered-network.py` passed.

Independent physical checks also passed for finite-compression/backpressure and pressure-rise arithmetic, activation-unit conversion, seven numerical binding-spectrum integrals, flux-weighted molecular energy, slab release and permeation time lag. A separate conservative explicit finite-volume calculation at three spatial resolutions checks the analytical absorbing-slab series and both face fluxes, with inventory closure and decreasing errors at four times. The permeation time-lag errors decrease approximately 1.44675e-4, 3.61684e-5 and 9.04170e-6 toward the L²/(6D) result. Six separately integrated gas/surface exchange cases agree with analytical decay rates and total removed/stored inventory. These checks establish the stated ideal models; primary equipment performance remains conditional evidence. Script `/tmp/check-gases-pumps-physics.py` passed.

